# aca-implement

Implement code and tests according to the design docs for the current scope
Change only code and tests, not design docs

## 1 Principles

1. **Goal-driven**
   Use confirmed delivery goals to determine the implementation scope and task priorities
   Use completion criteria and their verification evidence to determine whether the goals are met
2. **Dependency-driven parallelism**
   Use dependencies to determine the implementation order
   Run tasks in parallel wherever possible when their dependencies are satisfied and they have no shared resource conflicts
3. **Test-driven**
   Use external contracts and internal design to determine test expectations
   Drive implementation with tests and use both black-box and white-box tests to verify design requirements
4. **Scope constraints**
   Keep implementation and refactoring within the confirmed design scope

## 2 Workflow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start([Receive the confirmed design and implementation scope]) --> Plan["Goals and Task Orchestration · §3"]
    Plan --> Prepare["Implementation Preparation · §4"]
    Prepare --> Implement["Implementation and Integration · §5"]
    Implement -- All remaining tasks are blocked and no tasks are in progress --> Report
    Implement --> Modules{All module tasks are complete and integrated}
    Modules -- No --> Implement
    Modules -- Yes --> Verify["Overall Verification · §6"]
    Verify --> Complete{All completion criteria for the current scope are verified as satisfied}
    Complete -- No, the design is clear and fixes are possible within the current scope --> Implement
    Complete -- No, remaining verification can continue --> Verify
    Complete -- No, remaining fixes or verification are blocked --> Report
    Complete -- Yes --> Report(["Output and Next Step · §7"])
```

## 3 Goals and Task Orchestration

Read the design docs, project configuration, existing code, and tests for the current scope
Check the workspace state and recent commits to verify current implementation progress and existing changes
Preserve existing changes outside the scope of this implementation

1. List deliverables and their completion criteria based on the confirmed design and implementation scope
2. Compare delivery requirements with existing code and tests, and list what to reuse, modify, add, or remove
3. List the tests to run for this implementation based on the black-box and white-box test designs
   Specify the environments and fixtures these tests require
4. List the modules involved in this implementation and their dependencies based on the design
   Modules include submodules at all levels: top-level modules come from architecture, and submodules come from the internal composition of their direct parent's class-design
5. Specify whether each test uses real dependencies or test doubles based on the test design
   Check the readiness of required dependencies
6. Plan the source code and test file structure based on module boundaries, internal design, and repository conventions
   List all source code and test directories and files within the current implementation scope
7. Specify each file's responsibilities, owning module, and dependencies
   Check whether each file has a single responsibility and whether responsibilities overlap
   Adjust file boundaries when responsibilities are not singular or overlap
8. Split implementation preparation, module implementation, integration, and overall verification into tasks
   Use modules in the design as the smallest units of implementation, verification, and commits
   When submodules exist, create separate tasks for each submodule and the parent's composition implementation, with the parent task depending on all its submodule tasks
9. Specify each task's dependencies, owner, and completion criteria
10. Use the goal or task management capabilities provided by the current AI Agent environment to maintain the overall goal and task progress
    Record each task's status: pending implementation, in progress, complete, or blocked
    Record verification evidence and the next action for each task
    When resuming a task, check task records, the workspace, and recent commits
    Update task status based on the findings and continue implementation

## 4 Implementation Preparation

1. Add all missing source code and test directories and file placeholders for the current scope according to the plan
   Reuse existing files directly and create new files as empty files
   Delete files explicitly marked for removal in the plan only after verifying that they no longer serve a valid responsibility
   When a file's purpose, dependencies, or deletion scope cannot be confirmed, retain it and verify with the developer
   Start module implementation only after all file placeholders are in place
2. The main agent assigns separate workspaces to sub-agents
   Allocate test resources according to the test design to ensure parallel tasks do not interfere with each other
   Synchronize the prepared file structure to each workspace
3. Provide each sub-agent with the following task context
   - The overall implementation goal, the functionality to complete for the current module, and the scope of changes
   - Links to relevant chapters covering the structural basis, interfaces, internal design, and test cases for the current module and its dependencies
     The structural basis for top-level modules is architecture, and the structural basis for submodules is the internal composition of the parent's class-design
   - The readiness of each dependency and the arrangements for real dependencies or test doubles in tests
   - The workspace path, starting commit, files permitted for modification, and applicable repository conventions
   - Test environments and fixtures, verification commands, and module completion criteria
   - Change only code and tests
   - Follow the [Exception Handling](#52-exception-handling) requirements
   - Return changed files, commit records, verification commands and results, unverified items, and reasons for blockers

   When files owned by other tasks or shared files are involved, first ask the main agent to coordinate

## 5 Implementation and Integration

### 5.1 Implementation Loop

1. The main agent selects module tasks whose dependencies are satisfied
   Assign tasks that can run in parallel to sub-agents
2. Implement and verify each module according to the test design
   1. Prepare and run tests
      Reuse, write, or update black-box and white-box tests for the module's current scope according to the design
      When new or changed design requirements are not yet implemented, confirm that the corresponding tests fail
      For refactoring that preserves behavior, first run the corresponding tests to establish a verification baseline
   2. Complete and verify changes within the module's current scope according to the design
      Follow repository conventions for implementation details not specified by the design
      When the repository has no corresponding conventions, use the simplest implementation that meets the design
      When the design includes a Lark (Feishu) card mockup, implement it according to [Lark Card Mapping](~/.claude/skills/aca/skills/references/lark-card-mapping.md)
      Run module tests and confirm that all pass
   3. Refactor as needed and verify again
      After refactoring, rerun module tests and confirm that all pass
   When an unexpected test failure occurs and the design is clear, fix the code and rerun the affected tests
3. Check black-box and white-box test results and other completion criteria for the module's current scope
   Once all criteria are satisfied, stage and commit only the current task's changes
4. The main agent checks module commits and verification evidence
   Integrate the module's commits after confirming that it meets the completion criteria
   Run tests for affected modules and integration tests between modules on the integrated code
5. The main agent updates task status, verification evidence, and dependency readiness
   Record the reasons for blocked tasks and the conditions for resuming them
   Synchronize integrated dependency commits that have passed verification to module workspaces that require real dependencies
   If tasks remain that can be implemented, return to step 1
   If no tasks can be assigned and tasks are still in progress, wait for execution results
   After all module tasks are complete and integrated, proceed to [Overall Verification](#6-overall-verification)
   If all remaining tasks are blocked and no tasks are in progress, report the incomplete status according to [Output](#7-output)

### 5.2 Exception Handling

1. When the design is missing, contains conflicts, or cannot be implemented, the agent executing the task pauses affected tasks
   Sub-agents report the issue and its scope of impact to the main agent
   The main agent verifies the issue and its scope of impact, then reports them to the developer
   When the solution needs additions or revisions, recommend using `/aca-specify`
   Continue unaffected independent tasks
2. When issues are found in other repositories, the main agent verifies them and reports the issues and their impact to the developer
   Do not modify other repositories without authorization

## 6 Overall Verification

After all module tasks are complete and integrated, the main agent performs overall verification for the current delivery scope
Run module tests, integration tests, system tests, builds, and static checks according to the implementation plan
Execute the test cases specified in the design and verify the runtime results of the subject under test
Passing builds or static checks cannot replace behavioral test results

When issues are found for which the design is clear and fixes are possible within the current scope, return to [Implementation and Integration](#5-implementation-and-integration)
Rerun affected verification after fixes
Check each completion criterion against the overall goal
Update task records with verification results and unverified items

## 7 Output

When all completion criteria for the current scope are verified as satisfied, mark the goal and tasks as complete
If incomplete, report remaining work, reasons for blockers, and actions to resume
After this implementation is complete, use `/aca-review` to review the implementation within this scope
Provide Review with the overall goal, corresponding design, implementation scope, and comparison baseline
Identify the commits and uncommitted changes under review, and provide verification results and evidence
Use the following format for the final report, filling in the title and results according to the actual status

```markdown
# 🛠️ Implement: <complete / incomplete>

| Item | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Goal | <overall goal and completion criteria satisfaction> |
| Scope | <implementation scope and module progress> |
| Changes | <main implementation changes> |
| Verification | <test execution, build, and static check results> |
| Unverified | <unverified scope / none> |
| Commits | <commit records / uncommitted> |
| Blockers | <blockers and reasons / none> |
| Recommendations | <extension recommendations / none> |
| Next step | <one immediately actionable step> |
```